nce 2000, Goshen has set <strong class=\"sig\">28 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 89 that chance alone would give, and <strong>83 record highs</strong>, close to the 89 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (76). Goshen's all-time high is 111\u00b0F, on July 14, 1936.","t_chg":"Goshen's nights got warmer. Its days didn't.","p_chg":"Across 102 complete years: Goshen's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.36\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.28 to +0.45), about +4.0\u00b0F since 1916. Goshen's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.13 to +0.06) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 10 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; July and August fell.","t_rain":"Goshen has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Goshen's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.85 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.49 to +1.21), about +9.3 inches since 1916. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 39.4 inches a year. The wettest year was 1990 (52.73 in); the driest, 1930 (22.51 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}